\pard\qc\sa240\fs36\b\cf2 BNI Chapter Management System\par
\fs28 How to Make the Application Live\par
\fs20\b0 Deployment Tutorial\par
\pard\sa180\fs22\cf0
\b Document purpose\b0\par
This tutorial explains how to deploy the BNI Chapter Management System to a live server using Docker, a domain name, HTTPS, email, WhatsApp, persistent storage, and production security settings.\par
\par
\b 1. What you need\b0\par
- A Linux VPS from DigitalOcean, Hetzner, AWS Lightsail, Azure, or a similar provider.\par
- A domain name, for example bni.yourdomain.com.\par
- Access to the domain DNS settings.\par
- A Git repository containing the bni-system project.\par
- An SMTP email account.\par
- Optional: Meta WhatsApp Cloud API credentials.\par
- At least 2 GB RAM is recommended.\par
\par
\b 2. Prepare the domain\b0\par
Open your domain DNS manager and create an A record:\par
\f1 bni.yourdomain.com  ->  YOUR_SERVER_IP\f0\par
Wait for DNS propagation. You can check it with:\par
\f1 nslookup bni.yourdomain.com\f0\par
\par
\b 3. Connect to the VPS\b0\par
From Windows PowerShell or Terminal:\par
\f1 ssh root@YOUR_SERVER_IP\f0\par
Update the server:\par
\f1 apt update && apt upgrade -y\f0\par
Install Git, Docker, and the Docker Compose plugin:\par
\f1 apt install -y git ca-certificates curl\par
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh\par
apt install -y docker-compose-plugin\f0\par
Check installation:\par
\f1 docker --version\par
docker compose version\f0\par
\par
\b 4. Upload the application\b0\par
Clone the repository:\par
\f1 cd /opt\par
git clone YOUR_GIT_REPOSITORY_URL bni-system\
cd /opt/bni-system\f0\par
If the project is only on your computer, create a GitHub repository first, commit the project, and then use the clone command above.\par
\par
\b 5. Configure production secrets\b0\par
Open the Docker Compose file:\par
\f1 nano docker-compose.yml\f0\par
Replace development values with strong production values. The backend environment should include:\par
\f1 NODE_ENV: production\par
PORT: 4000\par
SERVER_MODE: LOCAL\par
DATABASE_URL: file:./data/bni.db\par
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET: REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_RANDOM_SECRET\par
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET: REPLACE_WITH_ANOTHER_LONG_RANDOM_SECRET\par
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN: 15m\par
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN: 7d\par
SMTP_HOST: smtp.your-email-provider.com\par
SMTP_PORT: 587\par
SMTP_USER: your-smtp-user\par
SMTP_PASS: your-smtp-password\par
SMTP_FROM: BNI Chapter <no-reply@yourdomain.com>\par
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID: YOUR_META_PHONE_NUMBER_ID\par
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN: YOUR_META_ACCESS_TOKEN\par
STORAGE_MODE: LOCAL\par
STORAGE_LOCAL_PATH: ./uploads\par
FRONTEND_URL: https://bni.yourdomain.com\f0\par
Never commit real passwords, SMTP passwords, or API tokens to Git. For stronger security, use a server-side .env file or Docker secrets.\par
\par
\b 6. Update the frontend URL\b0\par
The production frontend is served by the frontend container. Set:\par
\f1 FRONTEND_URL: https://bni.yourdomain.com\f0\par
The existing Nginx configuration forwards /api/ and /uploads/ requests to the backend container.\par
\par
\b 7. Build and start the application\b0\par
From /opt/bni-system run:\par
\f1 docker compose up -d --build\f0\par
Check running containers:\par
\f1 docker compose ps\f0\par
View logs:\par
\f1 docker compose logs -f backend\par
docker compose logs -f frontend\f0\par
The initial application will be available on port 8080:\par
\f1 http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:8080\f0\par
\par
\b 8. Create the initial demo data\b0\par
Run this only when setting up a new database:\par
\f1 docker compose exec backend npm run seed\f0\par
The seed script creates demo roles and accounts. Change every demo password after the first login.\par
\par
\b 9. Add HTTPS with Caddy\b0\par
Caddy is the easiest way to provide a domain and automatic HTTPS. Install it:\par
\f1 apt install -y debian-keyring debian-archive-keyring apt-transport-https curl\par
curl -1sLf 'https://dl.cloudsmith.io/public/caddy/stable/gpg.key' | gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/caddy-stable-archive-keyring.gpg\par
curl -1sLf 'https://dl.cloudsmith.io/public/caddy/stable/debian.deb.txt' | tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/caddy-stable.list\par
apt update && apt install -y caddy\f0\par
Create the Caddy configuration:\par
\f1 nano /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\f0\par
Use:\par
\f1 bni.yourdomain.com {\par
    reverse_proxy localhost:8080\par
}\f0\par
Restart Caddy:\par
\f1 systemctl reload caddy\f0\par
Open the live site:\par
\f1 https://bni.yourdomain.com\f0\par
Caddy will request and renew the TLS certificate automatically.\par
\par
\b 10. Configure email\b0\par
Use an SMTP provider such as Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, SendGrid, Mailgun, or Amazon SES. Add the SMTP values to the backend environment. Then restart:\par
\f1 docker compose up -d --build backend\f0\par
Test visitor registration and OTP delivery. If SMTP is blank, email is printed only in backend logs and is not sent to real users.\par
\par
\b 11. Configure WhatsApp\b0\par
Create a Meta Developer application and enable WhatsApp Cloud API. Add the phone number ID and access token to the backend environment. In the Control Panel, set the chapter WhatsApp contact number. Restart the backend after changing environment variables:\par
\f1 docker compose up -d --build backend\f0\par
Send a test OTP or notification. Check backend logs if delivery fails.\par
\par
\b 12. Configure the Control Panel\b0\par
Sign in as an administrator and open:\par
\f1 https://bni.yourdomain.com/app/control-panel\f0\par
Configure:\par
- Email sender address.\par
- WhatsApp contact number.\par
- Automatic notifications.\par
- Automatic visitor confirmation.\par
- Automatic OTP delivery.\par
- Automatic document refresh.\par
The workflow is automatic after configuration except for the two intentional manual checkpoints:\par
1. Admin/coordinator creates physical or virtual meetings.\par
2. Coordinator accepts or rejects payment proof.\par
\par
\b 13. Test the complete live workflow\b0\par
1. Open the public visitor registration page.\par
2. Register a visitor with a real email and WhatsApp number.\par
3. Log in to the visitor portal and verify editable profile details.\par
4. Admin creates a physical or virtual meeting.\par
5. Visitor registers for the meeting.\par
6. Confirm the OTP arrives by email and WhatsApp.\par
7. Coordinator displays the meeting QR code.\par
8. Visitor scans the QR code and completes attendance.\par
9. Visitor submits feedback and expression of interest.\par
10. Visitor uploads payment proof.\par
11. Coordinator manually verifies the payment.\par
12. Confirm automatic member creation and welcome email.\par
13. Confirm the member can update their photo, logo, Top Gives, Top Asks, introduction, and business details.\par
14. Confirm the presentation slide displays the member data.\par
\par
\b 14. Production data and uploads\b0\par
The Docker Compose file uses persistent volumes:\par
\f1 backend_data\par
backend_uploads\f0\par
Do not remove these volumes. They contain the SQLite database and uploaded photos, logos, payment proofs, and generated documents.\par
List volumes:\par
\f1 docker volume ls\f0\par
\par
\b 15. Backups\b0\par
Create a backup directory:\par
\f1 mkdir -p /opt/backups\f0\par
Stop writes briefly and copy the volumes or use a scheduled backup solution. For a simple database backup, first find the volume path:\par
\f1 docker volume inspect bni-system_backend_data\f0\par
Back up the database and uploads regularly. Store a second copy outside the VPS. Test restoring a backup before relying on it.\par
\par
\b 16. Updating the live application\b0\par
When new code is ready:\par
\f1 cd /opt/bni-system\
git pull\
docker compose up -d --build\
docker compose ps\f0\par
Check logs after every update:\par
\f1 docker compose logs --tail=100 backend\par
docker compose logs --tail=100 frontend\f0\par
\par
\b 17. Troubleshooting\b0\par
\b Site does not open\b0\par
Check DNS, firewall ports 80 and 443, Caddy status, and containers:\par
\f1 systemctl status caddy\par
docker compose ps\par
ufw allow 80/tcp\
ufw allow 443/tcp\f0\par
\par
\b API returns errors\b0\par
Check backend logs:\par
\f1 docker compose logs --tail=200 backend\f0\par
Confirm the backend is healthy:\par
\f1 curl http://localhost:4000/api/health\f0\par
\par
\b Email is not delivered\b0\par
Confirm SMTP host, port, username, password, and sender address. Check that the SMTP provider allows the sender address. Inspect backend logs.\par
\par
\b WhatsApp is not delivered\b0\par
Confirm the Meta access token, phone number ID, recipient format, WhatsApp business verification, and message template rules.\par
\par
\b Uploaded images are missing\b0\par
Confirm the backend upload volume exists and that Nginx forwards /uploads/ to the backend. Check:\par
\f1 curl -I https://bni.yourdomain.com/uploads/members/sample-member.svg\f0\par
\par
\b 18. Security checklist\b0\par
- Change all demo passwords.\par
- Use long random JWT secrets.\par
- Enable HTTPS before inviting users.\par
- Do not commit .env files or API tokens.\par
- Restrict SSH access and use firewall rules.\par
- Keep the operating system and Docker updated.\par
- Back up database and uploads daily.\par
- Use PostgreSQL when running multiple application servers.\par
- Test payment approval and role restrictions before launch.\par
\par
\b Demo accounts\b0\par
Admin: admin@bnichapter.test\par
Coordinator: coordinator@bnichapter.test\par
Member: member@bnichapter.test\par
Visitor: visitor@bnichapter.test\par
Demo password: Password@123\par
Change these credentials immediately on a live deployment.\par
\par
